# Bounded Authority Report Adapter — the full round-trip, in one notebook

This notebook proves the adapter works **end-to-end, self-contained, with no other project
running**. It plays all three roles of the capability model in-process so you can see exactly
what the adapter does, what it does **not** do, and how the pieces fit.

> The adapter has one job: it **signs a holder proof** binding an issuer-signed grant to a
> report. It does not issue grants, does not verify them, does not transport anything. This
> notebook mints a grant (playing the issuer) and verifies the result (playing a verifier) only
> to show the adapter's output is correct — those two roles are **not** the adapter's job in
> production.

## The model (three roles, three places)

```
ISSUER (the runtime)            HOLDER (the edge — THIS adapter)        VERIFIER (any party)
─────────────────────           ─────────────────────────────────       ─────────────────────
holds the issuer key            holds the holder key                    holds only PUBLIC keys
mints grants ahead of time  →   receives a grant, signs a proof     →   check_envelope(grant, proof)
                                over a specific report                  → :ok | {:error, :invalid}
```

This is DPoP (RFC 9449) applied to report authorization. The split is the security property:
the verifier never carries signing code; the issuer never sees each report; the holder proves
possession of a key bound to a grant, offline.

The **adapter is the HOLDER box** — that's all. The other two boxes are played here for the
demo so you can see the round-trip, but they live elsewhere in production (the runtime issues;
verifier application / any consumer verifies).

## Setup

```elixir
# Pulls the adapter (this repo) + its one dependency, bounded_authority_protocol (the public
# verifier/format package). First run fetches BAP from its private git remote (needs access).
adapter_path = __DIR__ |> Path.join("..") |> Path.expand()

Mix.install([
  {:bounded_authority_report_adapter, path: adapter_path}
])

alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1
```

## The key-handle contract (how the adapter touches a key without holding it)

The adapter never sees a private key. The caller supplies a `{module, term}` handle whose
module implements `sign/2` + `public_key/1`; the private bytes live behind those callbacks
(an HSM / KMS / key server in real life). Here it's a 4-line in-memory handle for the demo.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Handle do
  @moduledoc false
  def sign(message, {_pub, priv}),
    do: {:ok, :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :ed25519, message, [priv, :ed25519])}
  def public_key({pub, _priv}), do: {:ok, pub}
end
```

## Generate the two keypairs

```elixir
# ISSUER keypair — in production this lives in the bounded_authority runtime, NOT here.
{issuer_pub, issuer_priv} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, <<1::256>>)

# HOLDER keypair — in production this lives on the edge (the party that calls the adapter).
{holder_pub, holder_priv} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, <<2::256>>)
holder = {Demo.Handle, {holder_pub, holder_priv}}

now = System.system_time(:second)
```

## Role 1 — ISSUER: mint a grant  ⚠️ NOT the adapter's job

The runtime signs a grant that authorizes a holder (by the holder key's thumbprint) to perform
an operation. We do it here ONLY so the demo has a real grant to sign over.

```elixir
{:ok, holder_thumbprint} = V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(holder_pub, %{})

grant = %V1.Grant{
  key_id: "demo-issuer",
  issuer: "https://demo-issuer.test",
  grant_id: "urn:demo:grant:1",
  audiences: ["https://demo-verifier.test"],
  issued_at: now - 60,
  not_before: now - 60,
  expires_at: now + 3600,
  holder_thumbprint: holder_thumbprint,
  operations: [%V1.Operation{name: "report_demo", selectors: [:all]}]
}

{:ok, grant_input} = V1.grant_signing_input(grant, %{})
grant_sig = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :ed25519, grant_input.message, [issuer_priv, :ed25519])
{:ok, grant_compact} = V1.assemble_compact(grant_input, grant_sig)

"issuer-signed grant (#{String.length(grant_compact)} bytes): " <> String.slice(grant_compact, 0, 32) <> "…"
```

## Role 2 — HOLDER: the adapter signs the proof  ✅ THIS is the adapter's one job

The edge agent calls `sign_report/3` with the grant + the report fields + the holder key-handle.
The adapter builds the proof, signs it via the handle, verifies the signature against the
resolved holder key, and returns the `{grant, proof}` envelope. **It signs ONLY the proof —
never the grant.**

```elixir
report = %{
  grant_compact: grant_compact,
  operation: "report_demo",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://demo-verifier.test/report",
  invocation_id: "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
  # cast_arguments is BAP's tagged Json.value() form — in production this is
  # V1.Json.decode(raw_report_body_bytes) of the report body. A small value here for clarity.
  cast_arguments: {:object, [{"signal", {:string, "ok"}}]},
  nonce: "demo-nonce-1"
}

{:ok, %{grant: ^grant_compact, proof: proof}} =
  BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter.sign_report(report, holder, %{issued_at: now - 10})

"adapter proof (#{String.length(proof)} bytes): " <> String.slice(proof, 0, 32) <> "…"
```

## Role 3 — VERIFIER: check the envelope  (any party with the public protocol package)

The verifier (or any third party) verifies grant + proof against published keys, fully
offline. The verifier reconstructs what it expects the proof to bind, and `check_envelope`
checks both signatures + every binding.

```elixir
expected = %V1.ExpectedRequest{
  trusted_issuer: %V1.TrustedIssuer{key_id: "demo-issuer", public_key: issuer_pub},
  issuer: "https://demo-issuer.test",
  audience: "https://demo-verifier.test",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: "https://demo-verifier.test/report",
  invocation_id: "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
  operation: "report_demo",
  cast_arguments: {:object, [{"signal", {:string, "ok"}}]},
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  proof_max_age: 300,
  nonce: {:required, "demo-nonce-1"},
  bounds: V1.Bounds.maximum()
}

case V1.check_envelope(%V1.Credentials{grant: grant_compact, proof: proof}, expected) do
  {:ok, facts} -> "✅ VERIFIED — operation=#{facts.operation}, grant=#{String.slice(facts.grant_id, 0, 16)}…"
  {:error, :invalid} -> "❌ rejected"
end
```

## Now break it — a tampered proof is rejected

```elixir
[protected, payload, sig_b64] = String.split(proof, ".")
<<sig_bytes::binary-size(63), last>> = Base.url_decode64!(sig_b64, padding: false)
flipped = Base.url_encode64(<<sig_bytes::binary, Bitwise.bxor(last, 1)>>, padding: false)
tampered_proof = [protected, payload, flipped] |> Enum.join(".")

case V1.check_envelope(%V1.Credentials{grant: grant_compact, proof: tampered_proof}, expected) do
  {:ok, _} -> "❌ UNEXPECTED — a tampered proof verified (BUG)"
  {:error, :invalid} -> "✅ tampered proof rejected"
end
```

## And break it — a proof the grant was NOT bound to is rejected

A different holder key (the grant was issued to the original holder's thumbprint, not this one).

```elixir
{wrong_pub, wrong_priv} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519, <<9::256>>)
{:ok, %{proof: wrong_proof}} =
  BoundedAuthorityReportAdapter.sign_report(
    report,
    {Demo.Handle, {wrong_pub, wrong_priv}},
    %{issued_at: now - 10}
  )

case V1.check_envelope(%V1.Credentials{grant: grant_compact, proof: wrong_proof}, expected) do
  {:ok, _} -> "❌ UNEXPECTED — a wrong-key proof verified (BUG)"
  {:error, :invalid} -> "✅ wrong-key proof rejected"
end
```

## What just happened / what the adapter is (and isn't)

- **The adapter signed a proof** (Role 2) — that is its entire job. One function, `sign_report/3`.
- **The grant was minted by something else** (Role 1) — in production the `bounded_authority`
  runtime. The adapter takes the grant as an **input** and never signs it.
- **The verification was done by the public protocol package** (Role 3) — `check_envelope/2`,
  which any party (verifier application, a third party, this notebook) can call without depending on this
  adapter.
- **Tamper + wrong-key both fail closed** — the crypto bindings are real and non-vacuous.

So: a **self-contained signing library** that produces capability envelopes any public-package
verifier accepts. It is **not** standalone (it sits on the protocol package's format, by design)
and it is **not** a verifier or an issuer — it is the holder-side signer, reusable by whichever
party holds the right key.

> A real consumer (verifier application's report path) does **two more things** this demo skips because they
> are the *consumer's* job, not the adapter's: (a) bind the verified envelope to the authenticated
> reporter so a captured envelope can't be replayed cross-identity (see `docs/consumer-integration.md`
> §8); (b) dedupe nonces against a replay ledger (`§9`). The adapter produces the bytes; the
> consumer binds + persists + dedupes.
